  "query_preview": ".. \nIssued at 151 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nLow pressure over central Manitoba is expected to deepen as system \ncontinues to push east towards the Great Lakes.  Cold front \nassociated with this system has pushed into eastern South Dakota \ninto central Nebraska by 18Z.  Expect this boundary to continue to \nwork south and east, approaching northwest Missouri early this \nevening. Increasing moisture in the vicinity of 850 mb may be \nsufficient for a few showers and storms to develop along the \nboundary this evening.  Instability is fairly limited (generally 500-\n1000 J/KG), but increasing speed shear aloft may lead to stronger \nupdrafts capable of producing small hail. Low level convergence along \nthe front weakens through the evening as flow turns more west to \neast south of the boundary, which should lead to convection becoming \nincreasingly sparse through the overnight.  \n\nOrphaned boundary lingers across the forecast area tomorrow.  North \nof the boundary, expect northeast winds while southerly flow remains \nsouth of the boundary. Cooler airmass to the north will keep \ntemperatures in the 60s to lower 70s, while south of the boundary \ntemperatures are expected to approach 80 F. There could be enough \nconvergence along the front to potentially lead to an isolated late \nday shower/storm along the boundary, but lapse rates are near moist \nadiabatic aloft limiting any severe potential. \n\nLeading shortwave building east across Kansas tomorrow could lead to \nmore scattered shower activity developing across northwestern \nMissouri. A much larger and broad wave lifts north from Texas into \nthe Central Plains later on Friday leading to more widespread \nshower/thunderstorm activity Friday night into Saturday.  Halloween \nlooks to be fairly gloomy and cooler with scattered showers. \nPrecipitable water values 1.1-1.2\" (near daily max) should lead to \nfairly efficient rain processes. \n\nA series of phased waves are expected to build eastward late this \nweekend potentially developing into a cutoff low across the central \nUS through at least the firs half of next week.  This could lead to \ncooler more seasonal temperatures and potentially wet\nconditions.",
